\title{Series about an ordinary point}
\taxon{Example}
\date{2021-10-01}
\author{JB00}
\import{macros}

##{(1 - x^2)y'' - 2xy' + 2y = 0. \qquad (\dagger)}

\p{Expand around #{x = 0} (an ordinary point) [#{x = \pm 1} are RSPs]. Try #{y = \sum_{n=0}^\infty a_nx^n}, #{y' = \sum_{n=1}^\infty na_nx^{n-1}}, #{y'' = \sum_{n=2}^\infty n(n - 1)a_nx^{n-2}}. It is convenient to multiply (#{\dagger}) by #{x^2}:}

##{(1 - x^2)(x^2y'') - 2x^2(xy') + 2x^2(y) = 0.}

\p{Substituting:}

##{\sum_{n=2}^\infty a_n(1 - x^2)n(n - 1)x^n - 2\sum_{n=1}^\infty a_nx^2nx^n + 2\sum_{n=0}^\infty a_nx^{n+2} = 0.}

\p{Note: #{\sum_{n=0}^\infty a_nnx^{n+2} = \sum_{n=2}^\infty a_{n-2}(n - 2)x^n}. Equate coefficients of #{x^n} for #{n \geq 2}:}

##{\begin{gathered}
a_n(n - 1)n - a_{n-2}(n - 2)(n - 3) - 2a_{n-2}(n - 2) + 2a_{n-2} = 0 \\ \implies n(n - 1)a_n = n(n - 3)a_{n-2} \\ \implies a_n = \frac{n - 3}{n - 1}a_{n-2},
\end{gathered}}

\p{a recurrence relation. #{a_0} and #{a_1} are not fixed, and so are arbitrary constants.}

\p{Since #{a_3 = 0 \Rightarrow a_5 = 0 \Rightarrow a_7 = 0}, one solution is #{y = a_1x} (odd function of #{x}). The other, even solution: for #{n} even,}

##{\begin{gathered}
a_n = \frac{n - 3}{n - 1}a_{n-2} = \Big( \frac{n - 3}{n - 1} \Big)\Big( \frac{n - 5}{n - 3} \Big)a_{n-4} = \cdots = \frac{-1}{n - 1}a_0 \\ \implies y  = a_0\Big[ 1 - x^2 - \frac{x^4}{3} - \frac{x^6}{5} - \cdots \Big].
\end{gathered}}

\p{Note: #{\ln(1 \pm x) = \pm x - \frac{x^2}{2} \pm \frac{x^3}{3} - \cdots}, so}

##{y = a_0\Big[ 1 - \frac{x}{2}\ln\Big( \frac{1 + x}{1 - x} \Big) \Big],}

\p{a closed form solution. Note its behaviour near #{x = \pm 1}, which are RSPs; see later in the notes.}
